WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:13.000
Ten at night. A message from a teammate: I have not started my part. The presentation is tomorrow.

2
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.100
Sinta's thumbs are already typing something sharp.

3
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:25.200
But between the message and her reply there is a gap. It is about two seconds wide.

4
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.100
The stimulus is fixed. She did not choose it.

5
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.800
The gap is where she chooses.

6
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.500
And the response is entirely hers.

7
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.200
Ownership is not hustle or aggression. It is refusing to let the stimulus write your reply.

8
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.900
Her first draft: you always make me do everything. That sentence hands the pen to him.

9
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.900
She deletes it.

10
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:04.900
The second draft begins with I choose. She finishes slide four tonight, and asks for the last two by eight.

11
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:13.900
Your language is a live diagnostic. I have to, they made me, there is nothing I can do: these are reactive tells.

12
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:20.800
I choose, I will, here is what I can do: these are ownership tells.

13
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.300
Listen to yourself for one day, and count.

14
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:33.800
The next morning she does one thing nobody asked for: a one-page checklist for the group's next project.

15
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:41.900
Initiative inside your control needs no permission. That is exactly why interviewers ask for examples of it.

16
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.400
The stimulus is fixed. The response is yours.

17
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.500
Your words show who holds the pen.

18
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:59.400
Now it is your turn. Pick one frustrating moment from this week. Find the gap, and rewrite your reply.
